WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:08.160
<v Ira Glass>Years ago, the senior producer of our program, Brian Reed, worked for a few months in Homer, Alaska as a fish hauler. He still knows some people there.
<v Ira Glass>多年前，我们节目的高级制片人布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）曾在阿拉斯加州的荷马市做过几个月的鱼类搬运工。他至今在那儿还认识一些人。

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:22.870
<v Brian Reed>And that's actually how I heard about this. Basically, there's been this huge political fight going on in the town. Everyone's at each other's throats. They're going crazy on social media. They're up in arms. You know, my friend, who's lived there for decades, three decades, says she's never seen the town like this before.
<v Brian Reed>其实我就是这么听说这件事的。基本上，这个镇上发生了一场巨大的政治斗争。大家都在互相攻击。他们在社交媒体上表现得近乎疯狂。他们全都义愤填膺。你知道，我的朋友在那儿住了几十年，三十年了，她说她从来没见过这个镇子变成这样。

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.070
<v Ira Glass>And what's the issue?
<v Ira Glass>那问题出在哪里呢？

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:37.630
<v Brian  Reed>Well, it started over illegal immigration, which is a weird thing for Homer to be fighting over, because it doesn't really have any immigrants. Which makes sense, given where Homer is.
<v Brian  Reed>嗯，起因是非法移民问题，这对荷马市来说是个很奇怪的争论点，因为这里根本没有什么移民。考虑到荷马市的地理位置，这也在情理之中。

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.340
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, Homer's far.
<v Ira Glass>没错，荷马市很偏远。

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:44.560
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, it's literally the end of the road in America. That's like what they're known as.
<v Brian Reed>是的，它简直就是美国道路的尽头。大家就是这么称呼它的。

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.080
<v Ira Glass>What does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>那是什么意思？

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.760
<v Brian Reed>All right, so say like you were to drive, like cross over the Canadian border from Washington State, say.
<v Brian Reed>好吧，假设你要开车，比如说从华盛顿州穿过加拿大边境。

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.090
<v Ira Glass>OK, so I'm in Washington State. I've crossed into Canada.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我现在在华盛顿州。我已经进入加拿大了。

00:00:55.090 --> 00:01:07.170
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, you're driving north. You're driving for like 2,500 miles, like it's like basically the same length as crossing the US coast to coast. All right, so you go all the way up Canada that way. You cross back into Alaska. Can you picture that?
<v Brian Reed>对，你一路向北开。你要开大约2500英里，基本上相当于横跨美国东西海岸的距离。好吧，你就那样穿过整个加拿大。然后你再进入阿拉斯加。能想象那个画面吗？

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:07.720
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:20.210
<v Brian Reed>All right, then you got like another 11 hours. You're going to drive on to the peninsula, the Kenai Peninsula. You keep driving. And then it's right on this bay called the Kachemak Bay. And stretching out into the bay is this little strip of land called the spit.
<v Brian Reed>好的，然后你还要再开11个小时。你要开上基奈半岛（Kenai Peninsula）。你继续开。然后它就在这个叫卡切马克湾（Kachemak Bay）的海湾边上。伸进海湾里的是一小条陆地，叫做“沙咀”（the spit）。

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:20.900
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:28.690
<v Brian Reed>That's the end of the road. It's the furthest you can drive in America. And that's Homer.
<v Brian Reed>那就是道路的尽头。那是你在美国能开车到达的最远的地方。那就是荷马市。

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:37.890
<v Ira  Glass>What that means, is if you were to cross into the United States from Mexico, this is possibly the least convenient place in America you could end up.
<v Ira  Glass>这意味着，如果你从墨西哥进入美国，这可能是你在美国能到达的最不方便的地方。

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:57.210
<v Ira  Glass>If you're a member of ISIS, conceivably, I guess, you could fly to Alaska or cross the Bering Strait from Russia. But Brian actually checked with federal authorities, the official from US Immigration and Customs Enforcement who is responsible for Alaska, who said that this does not seem to be happening. And people in Homer know this. They know that illegal immigration is not their issue.
<v Ira  Glass>如果你是ISIS的成员，我想你可以想象，也许你可以飞到阿拉斯加或者从俄罗斯穿过白令海峡。但布莱恩实际上和联邦当局核实过，负责阿拉斯加地区的美国移民及海关执法局（ICE）官员表示，这种情况似乎并没有发生。荷马市的人也知道这一点。他们知道非法移民并不是他们的问题所在。

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.170
<v Brian Reed>Are you aware of any undocumented immigrants in Homer?
<v Brian Reed>你知道荷马市有任何无证移民吗？

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.010
<v Woman 1>No.
<v Woman 1>不知道。

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.950
<v Brian Reed>Do you have any memory of there being an undocumented immigrant in Homer?
<v Brian Reed>你记忆中有荷马市存在过无证移民吗？

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:04.500
<v Man 1>No.
<v Man 1>没有。

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.510
<v Brian Reed>Have you ever heard of an undocumented immigrant in Homer?
<v Brian Reed>你听说过荷马市有无证移民吗？

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:08.090
<v Woman 2>No.
<v Woman 2>没有。

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:13.130
<v Brian Reed>Have you ever heard of an undocumented immigrant being in Homer in your almost 40 years here?
<v Brian Reed>在你住在这里的近40年里，你听说过荷马市有无证移民吗？

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:13.630
<v Man 3>No.
<v Man 3>没有。

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:24.790
<v Brian Reed>I've asked pretty much everyone I talked to the same question. I heard a couple stories about some people in town who were undocumented at one point or another. But it's rare. I asked the Homer police chief about it. He sent me this email.
<v Brian Reed>我问了几乎所有我交谈过的人同样的问题。我听到过几个关于镇上有些人曾经是无证移民的故事。但这很罕见。我问了荷马市警察局长。他给我发了这封邮件。

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:36.470
<v Brian Reed>Quote, "Brian, Homer is not a destination for immigrants, illegal or legal, and it never has been. The Homer Police Department is not aware of any immigrants in our area. Mark Robel, Chief of Police, Homer Police Department."
<v Brian Reed>引述如下：“布莱恩，荷马市不是移民（无论是非法还是合法）的目的地，过去也从来不是。荷马市警察局不知道我们地区有任何移民。马克·罗贝尔（Mark Robel），荷马市警察局局长。”

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:37.770
<v Ira Glass>That seems pretty categorical.
<v Ira Glass>这看起来非常绝对。

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.210
<v Brian Reed>Yeah.
<v Brian Reed>是的。

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.450
<v Ira Glass>But people are still fighting over immigrants coming to their town?
<v Ira Glass>但人们还在为来到他们镇上的移民争吵？

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:48.080
<v Brian  Reed>Yeah, because it was mentioned in this resolution in the town council that would have welcomed immigrants to Homer.
<v Brian  Reed>是的，因为镇议会的一项决议中提到了这一点，该决议本可以欢迎移民来到荷马。

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.750
<v Protester  1>No, do we need a resolution?
<v Protester  1>不，我们需要一项决议吗？

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:50.780
<v Protester 2>--in our parking lot.
<v Protester 2>——在我们的停车场。

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.060
<v Protester 1>No, do we need a resolution?
<v Protester 1>不，我们需要一项决议吗？

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:52.680
<v Protester 2>Yeah.
<v Protester 2>是的。

00:02:52.680 --> 00:03:04.820
<v Brian Reed>This is two people from opposing sides of this issue. They're each holding opposing signs in their hands. One's red, one blue. They're standing on a street corner on Pioneer Avenue, the main drag in Homer. They've never met before. Arguing over this.
<v Brian Reed>这是两个持对立观点的人。他们手里各拿着相反的标语牌。一个是红色的，一个是蓝色的。他们站在先锋大道（Pioneer Avenue）的一个街角，那是荷马市的主要街道。他们以前从未见过面。就为了这个在争吵。

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:05.790
<v Protester 2>I think they need a--
<v Protester 2>我认为他们需要一个——

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.150
<v Protester 1>You'd like to see in a resolution--
<v Protester 1>你希望在决议中看到——

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.200
<v Protester 2>I would like to see an inclusive-- Everyone's welcome.
<v Protester 2>我希望看到一个包容的——欢迎所有人。

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.270
<v Protester 1>Please look--
<v Protester 1>请看看——

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.960
<v Protester 2>Everyone's welcome. Human rights are civil rights, civil rights are human rights.
<v Protester 2>欢迎所有人。人权就是公民权利，公民权利就是人权。

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:20.940
<v Protester 1>Do we have somebody saying that they're not welcome here? Don't we have an America where everybody's welcome? Why are you doing this? Why are you pushing this kind of crap?
<v Protester 1>我们有人说他们不受欢迎吗？难道我们不是一个欢迎所有人的美国吗？你为什么要这么做？你为什么要推行这种垃圾？

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.360
<v Ira Glass>So what got them to this point?
<v Ira Glass>那么是什么让他们到了这个地步？

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:44.720
<v Brian  Reed>All right, so basically, after the presidential election, liberals in town, they were not happy that Trump was elected. They were wanting to do something, and they drafted this resolution. And what it said, among other things, was that Homer stood against discrimination, and they mentioned undocumented immigrants and Muslims as people who would be welcome in the city.
<v Brian  Reed>好吧，基本上，总统大选后，镇上的自由派对特朗普当选感到不满。他们想做点什么，于是起草了这项决议。其中提到，荷马市反对歧视，他们提到了无证移民和穆斯林是这个城市欢迎的人群。

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.600
<v Ira Glass>So, they'd be welcome. Like, what does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>所以，他们会受到欢迎。那是什么意思？

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:56.830
<v Brian Reed>In practice, nothing. It was not a law. It was not even an ordinance. It was a resolution. It was just like a statement of how the town feels as a city, what it stands for.
<v Brian Reed>实际上，什么意思也没有。它不是法律。它甚至不是一项条例。它只是一项决议。它就像是一个城市对自身感受的声明，表明它的立场。

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.160
<v Ira Glass>So Trump voters saw that, and they felt--
<v Ira Glass>所以特朗普的支持者看到了，他们感到——

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:29.100
<v Brian Reed>They were pissed, because it was a slap in the face against their guy, basically. And an early version of this resolution actually called Trump out by name a bunch. So, here, I have a little bit of it here. It says like, "Whereas a new administration is in power in Washington, D.C., without a popular mandate, whereas during his campaign, President Donald Trump made statements offensive and harmful to the rights of women, immigrants, religious, racial, and ethnic minorities."
<v Brian Reed>他们很生气，因为这基本上是对他们支持的人的一记耳光。这项决议的早期版本实际上多次点名批评特朗普。看，我这儿有一点内容。它写道：“鉴于华盛顿特区的新政府并非基于民众授权，鉴于特朗普总统在竞选期间发表了冒犯并损害妇女、移民、宗教、种族和少数族裔权利的言论。”

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:37.970
<v Brian Reed>That kind of thing. And the early draft also said that the city would not always cooperate with federal agencies when it came to detaining undocumented immigrants.
<v Brian Reed>大概就是这类东西。早期草案还说，在拘留无证移民方面，市政府将不会总是配合联邦机构。

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:39.960
<v Ira Glass>Wow, so I see why Trump voters were mad.
<v Ira Glass>哇，所以我明白为什么特朗普的支持者会生气了。

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.950
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, and there's a lot of Trump voters in Homer. About half the city.
<v Brian Reed>是的，荷马市有很多特朗普的支持者。大约占城市的一半。

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.190
<v Ira Glass>So, and then this resolution came before the city council?
<v Ira Glass>所以，然后这项决议被提交到了市议会？

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:47.290
<v Brian Reed>Yeah.
<v Brian Reed>是的。

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:53.710
<v Council  Member>So at this time, we can take public comment on any matter on the agenda. Please come forward and sign in.
<v Council  Member>现在我们可以对议程上的任何事项进行公众评论。请上前登记。

00:04:53.710 --> 00:05:10.370
<v Brian Reed>People, like, descended on this town council meeting in droves, back in February. The council chambers were packed. People were lined up against the walls. They were sitting on the floor. They were lined up out the door and down the hall, into an overflow room, where they piped the meeting in. It was broadcast on the radio.
<v Brian Reed>二月份的时候，人们成群结队地涌向市议会会议。议会厅里挤满了人。人们沿着墙排队。他们坐在地板上。他们排到了门外，沿着走廊一直排到了一个溢出室，会议内容通过广播传到那里。会议还在电台上进行了直播。

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:26.180
<v Brian Reed>I talked to bunch of people-- people actually listened to it on the radio, all over town, which was amazing to me. It was intense. It was very emotional. Four people have cried as they were talking to me about this meeting, months after it happened. It was that emotional on both sides of the issue.
<v Brian Reed>我和好多人聊过——人们真的在收音机里听了会议，全镇都在听，这让我很惊讶。气氛很紧张。非常情绪化。有四个人在跟我谈论这次会议时哭了，尽管这已经是好几个月后的事了。双方在问题上都非常激动。

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.550
<v Ira Glass>Why was it so emotional?
<v Ira Glass>为什么会这么情绪化？

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:36.880
<v Brian Reed>I just think this meeting was kind of a moment where everyone in town declared for the first time where they stood on this, publicly, to their neighbors.
<v Brian Reed>我只是认为这次会议是一个时刻，镇上的每个人都第一次公开向邻居宣布了他们在这个问题上的立场。

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.610
<v Man At Meeting>If you vote yes on this, I'll be your worst nightmare. Because I'll push for a recall.
<v Man At Meeting>如果你对这个投赞成票，我将成为你最糟糕的噩梦。因为我会推动罢免。

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.090
<v Brian Reed>People at the meeting were overwhelmingly against the resolution.
<v Brian Reed>会议上的人绝大多数都反对这项决议。

00:05:48.090 --> 00:06:11.710
<v Man At Meeting>You bring in illegals, OK, by definition, they're criminals. OK, they live in the underworld. They don't have a stake in the game as we do. About the first time somebody gets raped or killed, I hope they come straight after the Homer City Council and sue the City to the point of bankruptcy. If you make this a magnet for that type of people, then, you know, shame on you.
<v Man At Meeting>你引进非法移民，好吗，根据定义，他们就是罪犯。好吗，他们生活在地下世界。他们不像我们这样对社区有归属感。如果第一次有人被强奸或杀害，我希望他们直接起诉荷马市议会，让城市破产。如果你让这里成为这类人的磁铁，那么，你们就丢脸吧。

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:13.410
<v Ira Glass>So people are really scared.
<v Ira Glass>所以人们真的很害怕。

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:28.870
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, and angry. And the people on the other side, they felt scared, seeing what their neighbors think and what they were willing to say-- because they felt like people were being hateful and discriminatory-- or what they said aloud at the meeting.
<v Brian Reed>是的，而且很愤怒。另一边的人则感到害怕，因为看到了他们的邻居在想什么，以及他们愿意说出什么——因为他们觉得人们在散布仇恨和歧视——或者他们在会议上大声说出的话。

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:36.900
<v Shamir>Three weeks ago, I stood on the corner of Heath and Pioneer with a sign that read Muslim immigrant, Homer local.
<v Shamir>三周前，我站在希思（Heath）和先锋路（Pioneer）的拐角处，举着一个写着“穆斯林移民，荷马本地人”的牌子。

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.100
<v Brian  Reed>This is a guy named Shamir. He's possibly the only Muslim in town.
<v Brian  Reed>这是一个叫沙米尔（Shamir）的人。他可能是镇上唯一的穆斯林。

00:06:41.100 --> 00:07:04.390
<v Shamir>In response, drivers made obscene hand gestures, called me a freak, and this same car whipped past three times to splash as much rain water on me as possible. I'm here to refute anyone who believes that we don't need this kind of resolution, because we don't have these kind of problems in Homer. Let me assure you that we do.
<v Shamir>作为回应，司机们做着淫秽的手势，叫我怪胎，同一辆车从我身边飞驰而过三次，尽可能多地把雨水溅到我身上。我在这里是为了驳斥任何认为我们不需要这种决议的人，因为我们在荷马没有这种问题。让我向你们保证，我们确实有。

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:15.590
<v Brian Reed>The resolution was voted down, but it didn't stop there. After that, there was a recall election which failed. That whole email scandal. The ACLU got involved with a lawsuit against the city.
<v Brian Reed>这项决议最终被否决了，但事情并没有就此结束。之后，举行了一场罢免选举，但失败了。还有整个邮件丑闻。美国公民自由联盟（ACLU）介入了对城市的诉讼。

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:24.240
<v Ira Glass>OK, so here's what I'm getting from this. People are really mad at each other and they are not stopping this conflict, and it is all about immigration.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我从这里得到的信息是这样的。人们对彼此非常愤怒，他们没有停止冲突，而这一切都是关于移民问题的。

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.030
<v Brian Reed>That's how it started at least, yeah.
<v Brian Reed>至少这就是开始，是的。

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:30.570
<v Ira  Glass>In this town that doesn't actually have an immigration problem.
<v Ira  Glass>在这个实际上并没有移民问题的镇子上。

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:51.060
<v Brian Reed>Exactly. And actually, you know, I pointed this out to people in Homer as I talked to them. And you know, I get all sorts of different reactions. Like some people definitely saw the absurdity in it. Some people thought it wasn't crazy to think that if that resolution was passed, or if Homer somehow became a sanctuary city, they thought, sure, undocumented immigrants or refugees could totally start coming here.
<v Brian Reed>正是如此。而且，你知道，我在和荷马市的人交谈时向他们指出了这一点。你知道，我得到了各种不同的反应。有些人确实看到了它的荒谬之处。有些人认为，如果该决议通过，或者荷马以某种方式成为了庇护城市，那么无证移民或难民完全可能开始来到这里，这种想法并不疯狂。

00:07:51.060 --> 00:08:03.280
<v Brian Reed>And then plenty-- you know, a lot of people told me like, yeah, this is not a fight, actually, over immigration. It's over something bigger. And I talked to this one woman, Hannah. She's a boat captain. She put it, I don't know-- I thought the best of anybody. She said this.
<v Brian Reed>然后很多人——你知道，很多人告诉我，是的，这实际上不是一场关于移民的斗争。它关乎更大的问题。我和一位女士聊过，汉娜（Hannah）。她是一位船长。我不知道——我觉得她总结得最好。她是这么说的。

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:33.640
<v Hannah>It's like we're acting out this play that reflects our deepest anxieties, but none of it's based on anything real. It's based on what-ifs. It's based on I don't think the same way as you. Things that could happen, things that have happened in other places, fear. And the fear of something becoming real. But none of it is based on things that are actually happening here.
<v Hannah>这就像我们在演一出戏，反映了我们内心深处的焦虑，但这一切都不是基于任何真实的东西。它是基于“如果……怎么办”。它基于“我的想法和你不一样”。那些可能发生的事情，那些在其他地方发生过的事情，恐惧。以及对某些事情变成现实的恐惧。但没有任何东西是基于这里正在发生的真实事情。

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:54.360
<v Ira Glass>Right now, all over this country, lots of people are wary of immigrants, wanting something done about immigrants, they're fearful of immigrants, whether or not there are immigrants in their lives. Today on our program, we have stories of two places where people got really upset about immigrants, even though immigration did not seem like it was the most important thing there going on at all.
<v Ira Glass>现在，在这个国家的各个地方，很多人都对移民保持警惕，想要对移民采取行动，他们害怕移民，无论他们的生活中是否真的有移民。今天我们的节目中，我们有两个地方的故事，那里的民众对移民非常不满，尽管移民似乎根本不是当地最重要的事情。

00:08:54.360 --> 00:09:27.220
<v Ira Glass>One of these places is Homer, a small town that is about as remote as you can get-- as you've heard-- with almost no immigrants. The other one's in the suburbs, in the densely developed east coast-- Rockville, Maryland, with lots of immigrants, documented and undocumented. And what's interesting in both places is that we see why people's feelings on this issue just spiraled bigger, and bigger, and bigger, beyond the facts at hand. From WBEZ in Chicago, it's This American Life. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>其中一个地方是荷马市，正如你所听到的，这是一个极其偏远的小镇，几乎没有移民。另一个在郊区，在人口稠密的东海岸——马里兰州的罗克维尔（Rockville），那里有大量的移民，包括合法和无证移民。有趣的是，在这两个地方，我们都能看出为什么人们在这个问题上的情绪超出了现有事实，变得越来越激烈。这里是WBEZ芝加哥电台的《美国生活》（This American Life）。请继续收听。

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:47.200
<v Ira  Glass>Act One, Fear. So Brian spent some time in Homer, Alaska talking to all kinds of people involved in this debate that the town has been having over whether it should welcome immigrants or not. And he became especially interested in this one guy.
<v Ira  Glass>第一幕，恐惧。布莱恩在阿拉斯加州的荷马市待了一段时间，与所有参与这场争论的人进行了交谈，讨论这个镇子是否应该欢迎移民。他对比这一个人特别感兴趣。

00:09:47.200 --> 00:10:09.090
<v Brian Reed>I found this guy, because he testified at that Homer City Council meeting, the one where everyone was so upset. I spoke to lots of people who attended the meeting, or listened to it. What everyone had in common was that they all seemed so sure of their sides, like of course I support the ideas in this resolution, or of course I don't. Know one sounded uncertain about it. Until I met this guy.
<v Brian Reed>我找到了这个人，因为他在那次荷马市议会会议上作了证，就是大家都非常激动的那次。我采访了许多参加会议或收听了会议的人。他们共同点是，他们似乎都非常确信自己的立场，比如我当然支持这项决议中的观点，或者我当然不支持。没有人听起来对此感到不确定。直到我遇到了这个人。

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:22.260
<v Ben Tyrer>Believe it or not, I'm kind of an emotional person. When I see people in conflict with each other, it just kind of upsets me, and I'd really rather not deal with it, so I don't have to take any more antacids.
<v Ben Tyrer>信不信由你，我其实是个很感性的人。当我看到人们发生冲突时，我就会感到心烦，我真的不想去处理这些事，这样我就不用再吃更多的抗酸剂了。

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:27.430
<v Brian Reed>This is Ben Tyrer, who generally avoids politics and news for that very reason.
<v Brian Reed>这是本·泰勒（Ben Tyrer），正是因为这个原因，他通常会避开政治和新闻。

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:30.170
<v Ben Tyrer>News stresses me out, if we're being honest.
<v Ben Tyrer>老实说，新闻让我感到压力。

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:31.100
<v Brian Reed>I hope we are.
<v Brian Reed>我希望我们是坦诚的。

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:31.690
<v Ben Tyrer>Yeah.
<v Ben Tyrer>是的。

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:46.310
<v Brian Reed>Ben Tyrer, jazz musician and janitor at Kenai Peninsula College, and to me, the most interesting person in town, because he's the only person I met who seemed to actually be struggling to figure out what side to be on. Ben was born and raised in Homer.
<v Brian Reed>本·泰勒，爵士乐手，基奈半岛学院的清洁工。对我来说，他是镇上最有趣的人，因为他是我见过的唯一一个似乎在努力弄清楚自己该站在哪一边的人。本是在荷马土生土长的。

00:10:46.310 --> 00:11:05.620
<v Brian Reed>Now he's 27, married, with a two-year-old daughter, which has given him a talent for dad humor that's ahead of his years. My favorite joke from his Facebook page-- "What comes after Colin Firth? Colin Thecond." When all these people in Homer were suddenly up in arms over whether to welcome immigrants, Ben didn't want to jump to any unfair conclusions.
<v Brian Reed>现在他27岁，已婚，有一个两岁的女儿，这让他拥有了一种超越年龄的冷幽默天赋。我在他Facebook页面上最喜欢的笑话是——“Colin Firth（科林·费尔斯）后面是什么？Colin Thecond（科林·第二）”。当荷马市所有人都突然为是否欢迎移民而义愤填膺时，本不想草率地得出不公平的结论。

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:28.760
<v Ben Tyrer>I'm not just going to willy-nilly make generalizations about an entire group of people based on my feelings. We really have to be careful of that kind of thing, because in the past, that has led to some pretty unpleasant consequences. Before I make a decision like that, I really need to be sure that I am coming from what I consider to be an informed place.
<v Ben Tyrer>我不会仅仅凭自己的感觉就随意地对整个群体进行概括。我们真的必须小心这种事情，因为在过去，这导致了一些非常不愉快的后果。在做出那样的决定之前，我真的需要确保我是从我认为知情的位置出发的。

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:47.580
<v Brian Reed>Ben considers himself a moderate. Says he's voted pretty evenly for both parties. Last year, he was into Bernie Sanders. But when the presidential election came around, he ended up just sitting it out, because he found it too unpleasant. He says he never paid attention to immigration as an issue. It was not something that really crossed his mind until the resolution.
<v Brian Reed>本认为自己是个温和派。他说他在两个党派之间投得相当均匀。去年，他喜欢伯尼·桑德斯（Bernie Sanders）。但当总统大选到来时，他最终还是选择了弃权，因为他觉得这太令人不快了。他说他从未关注过移民问题。直到那项决议出来，这都不是他真正在意的事情。

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:54.630
<v Brian Reed>It was all over social media, being talked about all around town. So he read it. And it scared him.
<v Brian Reed>社交媒体上到处都在谈论这件事，全镇也都在谈论。所以他读了它。这让他感到害怕。

00:11:54.630 --> 00:12:08.650
<v Brian Reed>Ben describes the feeling he got as an amorphous blob of fear. That's what crept up inside him. And he immediately thought of this story he'd stumbled across a few weeks before, maybe in his Facebook feed, or just poking around online, about this one attack in Europe.
<v Brian Reed>本形容他得到的感觉是一种模糊的恐惧团块。这就是在他心里蔓延的感觉。他立刻想起了几周前他在网上偶然发现的一个故事，可能是在他的Facebook订阅里，或者只是在网上乱逛时看到的，关于欧洲发生的一次袭击。

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:13.140
<v Ben Tyrer>Cologne. It was Cologne that happened in Germany, the New Year's thing.
<v Ben Tyrer>科隆。那是发生在德国的科隆，新年发生的那个事件。

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:37.150
<v Brian Reed>It was an incident from New Year's, the year before, 2016, when hundreds of women in Cologne, Germany filed reports with the police saying they'd been sexually assaulted while they were in big crowds during celebrations. Many of the women said that the men who'd assaulted them were Arabic speakers and appeared to be from North Africa. It was a very disturbing event which had shaken Germany, and it shook Ben too.
<v Brian Reed>那是前一年，即2016年新年发生的一起事件，当时德国科隆有数百名妇女向警方报案，称她们在庆祝活动的人群中遭到性侵。许多妇女说，袭击她们的男人说着阿拉伯语，看起来像是来自北非。这是一起非常令人不安的事件，震惊了德国，也震惊了本。

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:45.450
<v Ben Tyrer>Wow, in Europe? Of all places, this is happening in Europe? Like I said, I hadn't really been paying attention. And it just shocked me.
<v Ben Tyrer>哇，在欧洲？在所有地方中，这竟然发生在欧洲？就像我说的那样，我并没有真正关注。这让我感到震惊。

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:52.250
<v Brian Reed>So you hadn't followed the news through like the attacks in Paris, or the Charlie Hebdo attacks? You just hadn't been watching the news on that stuff?
<v Brian Reed>那你没有关注像巴黎袭击或查理周刊袭击那样的新闻吗？你只是没有关注这些事？

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:52.700
<v Ben Tyrer>No.
<v Ben Tyrer>没有。

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:54.420
<v Brian Reed>I'm not judging you, I'm just asking.
<v Brian Reed>我不是在评判你，我只是在问。

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.810
<v Ben Tyrer>No, like I said, no.
<v Ben Tyrer>没有，就像我说的，没有。

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:10.910
<v Brian  Reed>So that's what was fresh in Ben's mind when he read the resolution-- Cologne. And it freaked him out.
<v Brian  Reed>所以这就是本在读到那项决议时脑海中挥之不去的记忆——科隆事件。这让他吓坏了。

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:33.710
<v Brian  Reed>Even though Ben admits that immigration is not exactly the biggest issue in his town, no one is sharpening their knives and thinking of Homer, he tells me. And even though much of the resolution was about fending off discrimination and protecting vulnerable people, things Ben agrees with in principle, when it came to welcoming undocumented immigrants, to Ben it seemed like the city might be opening a door to people who could possibly do harm.
<v Brian  Reed>尽管本承认移民问题在他们镇上算不上最大的问题，没有人在磨刀霍霍地想着荷马市，他告诉我。尽管那项决议大部分内容是关于抵制歧视和保护弱势群体——本原则上同意这些观点——但在欢迎无证移民的问题上，本觉得这个城市可能会给那些可能造成伤害的人敞开大门。

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:54.040
<v Brian  Reed>Everyone around him was so riled up about this. It felt irresponsible not to have an informed opinion on it. So Ben started staying up late, night after night, spending hours on the computer long after his wife went to bed, doing something I imagine few of the millions of people who are up late on the internet every night are doing-- researching global migration.
<v Brian  Reed>周围的每个人都为此感到愤怒。不对此发表知情意见似乎是不负责任的。所以本开始熬夜，一晚又一晚，在妻子睡觉后，在电脑前花了几个小时，做了一件我想象中每晚在网上熬夜的数百万人中很少有人做的事——研究全球移民问题。

00:13:54.040 --> 00:14:13.450
<v Brian  Reed>He doesn't have a printer. So he'd go back and forth to his parents' house, which is right in front of his place, to print out articles and reports. Ben may not be a big news guy, but he does like to research. He's been slowly building the house he and his family now live in, pretty much singlehandedly, with no experience in construction, based largely off research he's done online.
<v Brian  Reed>他没有打印机。所以他来回奔波于他父母的家，那就在他住处前面，去打印文章和报告。本可能不是个新闻发烧友，但他确实喜欢做研究。他现在住的房子是他和家人几乎单枪匹马建起来的，没有任何建筑经验，主要就是靠他自己在网上做的研究。

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:21.200
<v Brian  Reed>So he figured he could take those research skills and apply them to this question of whether immigration is a good thing for a place to encourage or bad.
<v Brian  Reed>所以他想他可以用这些研究技能来处理这个问题：鼓励移民来到这里是好事还是坏事。

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:24.670
<v Brian Reed>And did you feel like you had an open mind about it? Did you feel like--
<v Brian Reed>你觉得你对这件事心态开放吗？你觉得——

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:45.580
<v Ben Tyrer>If we're being honest, I didn't have an open mind about it. But I knew that, and I knew that that's not a good thing to have if you are trying to have an actual view of what's going on in the world instead of just what you want to have going on in the world. So I kind of took it upon myself to question-- actually question-- everything that I think.
<v Ben Tyrer>如果我们坦诚的话，我的心态并不开放。但我知道这一点，我也知道，如果你试图对世界上正在发生的事情有一个真实的看法，而不是仅仅想要发生什么，这种心态是不好的。所以我自己承担了质疑——实际上是质疑——我所想的一切的责任。

00:14:45.580 --> 00:15:05.970
<v Brian Reed>Ben set out to do something many of us believe we've done in our own lives, but I think, if we're being honest, rarely actually have. He set out to do what a good citizen in a democracy is supposed to do-- delve into the facts, aware of your preconceptions, but with as much of an open mind as possible and form a knowledgeable opinion based on the evidence.
<v Brian Reed>本着手去做许多人相信我们在自己的生活中已经做过的事情，但我想，如果我们坦诚的话，很少有人真正做到过。他着手做了一名民主制度下的优秀公民应该做的事情——深入挖掘事实，意识到自己的成见，但尽可能以开放的心态，根据证据形成知情的意见。

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:22.810
<v Brian Reed>And when he did that, it took him to a place that really surprised him, and made him question how he views the world. Ben opens his laptop and tries to replicate for me best he can remember how he began. He started with a simple Google search-- something like "What's the refugee situation in Europe?"
<v Brian Reed>当他这样做时，这把他带到了一个真正让他惊讶的地方，并让他质疑自己看待世界的方式。本打开笔记本电脑，尽量向我还原他开始时的样子。他从一个简单的谷歌搜索开始——类似于“欧洲的难民情况如何？”

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:34.200
<v Ben Tyrer>Migrant crisis-- migration to Europe explained in seven charts. The EU and the refugee crisis. They like to use the word crisis a lot, which was kind of a red flag.
<v Ben Tyrer>移民危机——用七张图表解释欧洲的移民问题。欧盟和难民危机。他们喜欢用“危机”这个词，这算是一个警示信号。

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:49.980
<v Brian Reed>And before he knew it, he was down a rabbit hole of frightening stories-- stories about "no go zones" outside Paris. Immigrant neighborhoods, where native French people supposedly could no longer enter. He bumped into more stories about the sexual assaults in Cologne.
<v Brian Reed>在他意识到之前，他就掉进了令人恐惧的故事兔子洞里——关于巴黎郊外“禁区”（no-go zones）的故事。那里是移民社区，据说法国本地人已经无法进入了。他又看到了更多关于科隆性侵案的故事。

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:55.140
<v Brian Reed>[WOMAN SPEAKING GERMAN IN VIDEO CLIP]
<v Brian Reed>[视频片段中一名妇女用德语讲话]

00:15:55.140 --> 00:16:16.700
<v Ira  Glass>Watching and reading all this stuff, late at night, in the dark Alaskan winter, trying to forge an understanding of a place very far away that Ben has never been-- Europe-- and of people from even further away than that, that he has never met, Ben felt upset and worried. But also not entirely sure whether he could trust the information he was getting.
<v Ira  Glass>在黑暗的阿拉斯加冬天，深夜看着和读着这一切，试图弄清楚一个非常遥远、本从未去过的地方——欧洲——以及比那更遥远、本从未见过的人们，本感到心烦意乱，忧心忡忡。但也并不完全确定他是否能相信他得到的信息。

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:28.200
<v Ben Tyrer>The immediate gut reaction was, that's awful. But it's also like, OK, they're using these specifically to get that gut reaction out of people, to really get people to one side.
<v Ben Tyrer>即时的直觉反应是，那太糟糕了。但同时也觉得，好吧，他们正利用这些东西来激起人们的这种直觉反应，真正地把人们引向一边。

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:37.600
<v Brian  Reed>You've got that kind of sub-narrative or something going on in your head, or sub commentary as you're reading through this stuff? Where's this coming from? What's the agenda of it?
<v Brian  Reed>你脑子里有那种次级叙事或类似的东西，或者当你阅读这些东西时有次级评论吗？这是从哪里来的？它的议程是什么？

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:47.070
<v Ben Tyrer>Exactly. Who is benefiting from me seeing this? Is it purely altruistic? Or is there something else going on?
<v Ben Tyrer>正是如此。谁从我看到这些东西中受益？这是完全无私的吗？还是另有隐情？

00:16:47.070 --> 00:17:03.360
<v Brian  Reed>So Ben sought out information from other perspectives. He watched interviews with refugees and asylum seekers, telling their stories firsthand, of fleeing persecution or war in their home countries and embarking on harrowing journeys to Europe.
<v Brian  Reed>所以本寻求了其他视角的各种信息。他观看了难民和寻求庇护者的采访，听他们讲述第一手故事，讲述如何从家乡逃离迫害或战争，并踏上前往欧洲的惊心动魄的旅程。

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:14.920
<v Ben Tyrer>This guy, he seems like a nice guy who was just trying to do the right thing. You know, I'd be more than willing to help a person like that. He's a very brave man.
<v Ben Tyrer>这个人，他看起来是个好人，只是试图做正确的事。你知道，我更愿意帮助像那样的人。他是个非常勇敢的人。

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:18.010
<v Brian Reed>But then he'd feel unsure about that stuff too.
<v Brian Reed>但随后他对那些东西也感到不确定。

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:33.760
<v Ben Tyrer>If you watch this stuff kind of stuff enough for a long enough period of time, there's almost this paranoid reaction that you get. Is this real? Is this staged? It's a really dark, strange place you get to in your own head when you watch enough of these.
<v Ben Tyrer>如果你看这种东西看的时间够长，你几乎会产生一种偏执的反应。这是真的吗？这是演出来的吗？当你看了足够多这些东西时，你的脑子里会进入一个非常黑暗、奇怪的地方。

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:47.030
<v Brian Reed>Remember, Ben was new to news. I was basically a baby learning to walk, he tells me. His understanding was that publications like The New York Times and The Washington Post had a bad rap, so he didn't really spend too much time there.
<v Brian Reed>记住，本是个新闻初学者。我基本上是个刚学走路的婴儿，他告诉我。他的理解是，《纽约时报》和《华盛顿邮报》这类出版物声名狼藉，所以他并没有真正在那里花太多时间。

00:17:47.030 --> 00:18:03.040
<v Brian Reed>He would go to a site like the BBC, but worried maybe they were giving him a liberal bent. So then he'd go to a site like this conservative Canadian one he found, The Rebel. But he knew he couldn't fully trust that either. The whole experience was head spinning. So he started digging up primary sources as well.
<v Brian Reed>他会去像BBC这样的网站，但担心也许他们会给他一个自由派的偏见。所以他会去像他发现的这个保守派加拿大网站，《叛逆者》（The Rebel）。但他知道他也不能完全信任那个。整个经历让人头晕目眩。所以他也开始挖掘主要来源。

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:21.070
<v Brian Reed>He's got a bunch of papers that he's printed out for me, documents and reports that he's compiled. He went to Cornell Law School's website to read up on immigration law, trying to figure out if sanctuary cities were legal. He determined that they were not. He also pulled crime stats directly from the German Ministry of the Interior.
<v Brian Reed>他给我打印了一堆文件，他汇编的文档和报告。他去了康奈尔法学院的网站查询移民法，试图弄清楚庇护城市是否合法。他断定它们是不合法的。他还直接从德国联邦内政部调取了犯罪统计数据。

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:29.140
<v Brian Reed>He did that after reading this one article which he shows me with the headline, "Report, Migrants Committing Disproportionately High Crime in Germany."
<v Brian Reed>他是在读了这篇文章后这样做的，他向我展示了标题——“报告：移民在德国犯下的罪行高得不成比例”。

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:30.750
<v Brian Reed>What source is that from?
<v Brian Reed>那个来源是哪里的？

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:35.430
<v Ben Tyrer>This is-- oh, Breitbart.
<v Ben Tyrer>这是——哦，布赖特巴特（Breitbart）。

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:56.170
<v Brian Reed>Breitbart, the right wing nationalist site formerly run by Donald Trump's chief strategist, Steve Bannon, which is proudly anti-immigration. The article states that crime rates would have remained static in Germany if not for migrants coming in-- that the country recorded an extra 402,000 crimes by immigrants since 2014.
<v Brian Reed>布赖特巴特，那个曾由特朗普首席策略师史蒂夫·班农（Steve Bannon）运营的右翼民族主义网站，它自豪地反对移民。文章指出，如果不是因为移民涌入，德国的犯罪率本会保持不变——自2014年以来，该国记录了40.2万起由移民犯下的额外罪行。

00:18:56.170 --> 00:19:04.630
<v Brian Reed>How do you feel about Breitbart? I mean, that's a place people have a lot of feelings about, in terms of as a news source. I'm curious what you feel?
<v Brian Reed>你对布赖特巴特感觉如何？我是说，作为一个新闻来源，人们对那个地方有很多感受。我很好奇你的感受？

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:12.800
<v Ben      Tyrer>Yeah. If I had only used Breitbart, I would have been a little bit more suspicious.
<v Ben      Tyrer>是的。如果我只用了布赖特巴特，我会更怀疑一点。

