<v Brian Reed>我不明白袭击是有组织的，还是更像一种自发的暴民行为。此外，在布赖特巴特这样的网站上有标题，比如“新年强奸恐怖”和“强奸难民不受欢迎”。我不知道该怎么想。

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:36.690
<v Brian Reed>But Damien explained these attacks were real. They happened. He reported on them. There were in fact about 1,000 criminal complaints filed with Cologne Police, about half of those for various levels of sexual assault, including allegations of rape. The other half include theft of purses and phones, and other violence too.
<v Brian Reed>但达米安解释说这些袭击是真实的。它们发生了。他报道了它们。事实上，科隆警方收到了约1000起刑事投诉，其中约一半是针对不同程度的性侵犯，包括强奸指控。另一半包括偷窃钱包和手机，还有其他暴力行为。

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:59.760
<v Brian Reed>There are terrifying accounts of women being surrounded and attacked by men. Damien thinks it's important to understand the pandemonium in German city centers on New Year's Eve, which he says is unlike public parties elsewhere in Europe. There are huge masses of people. It's dark. People were setting off fireworks in the crowd. Damien says what occurred was somewhere between a wild drunken party and a riot that police couldn't control.
<v Brian Reed>有关于妇女被男人包围和袭击的可怕叙述。达米安认为理解新年前夜德国市中心的混乱非常重要，他说这与欧洲其他地方的公共派对不同。有大量的人群。天很黑。人们在人群中燃放烟花。达米安说发生的事情处于疯狂的酒后派对和警察无法控制的暴乱之间。

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:21.930
<v Damien Mcguinness>And the drunkenness on the streets is of a level I don't think you'd really see in the states. And it's not to minimize the trauma that these women underwent, or to say it's any less shocking. But it goes to explain why the police found it hard to control, and helps explain why then criminal elements took advantage of that to do something appalling. It makes it seem less crazy.
<v Damien Mcguinness>街头的醉酒程度是我认为你在美国真的看不到的。这并不是要淡化这些妇女遭受的创伤，或者说这就不那么令人震惊了。但这有助于解释为什么警方发现很难控制，并有助于解释为什么犯罪分子利用这一点做了一些骇人听闻的事情。这让它看起来不那么疯狂。

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:43.560
<v Brian Reed>Most of the suspects were identified. About 150 men, were immigrants and asylum seekers, particularly from North African countries. There are accounts from people who were there of many more men being involved. So what Ben saw about Cologne, while it was sensationalized with words like rape-fugees, was, for the most part, true.
<v Brian Reed>大多数嫌疑人被确定身份了。大约150名男子是移民和寻求庇护者，特别来自北非国家。有现场的人描述有更多的男人参与其中。所以本看到的关于科隆的内容，虽然用“强奸难民”这样的词进行了炒作，但大部分是真实的。

00:30:43.560 --> 00:31:10.600
<v Brian Reed>Next, Ben and Damien and I looked at one of the Breitbart articles that made a big impression on Ben. It starts, quote, "A massive migrant crime wave is surging across Germany," end quote, citing a report from the interior ministry that's over 100 pages long and entirely in German. The article says Germany recorded roughly 402,000 crimes committed by migrants in 2015. Damien read the underlying report, and he says that 402,000 number--
<v Brian Reed>接下来，本、达米安和我看了其中一篇让本印象深刻的布赖特巴特文章。它开头引用道：“大规模移民犯罪浪潮正在德国蔓延”，引述了一份长达100多页且完全是德语的内政部报告。文章称，2015年德国记录了大约40.2万起由移民犯下的罪行。达米安读了底层报告，他说那40.2万的数字——

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:14.060
<v Damien Mcguinness>That is correct. That's a correct figure.
<v Damien Mcguinness>那是正确的。那是个正确的数字。

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:15.050
<v Brian Reed>Except--
<v Brian Reed>除了——

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:35.720
<v Damien Mcguinness>This 400,000 figure is actually including the crime of crossing the border as an asylum seeker. Now by definition, that can only be committed by migrants, and in fact, is how most refugees, by legitimately accepted international law, actually apply for asylum.
<v Damien Mcguinness>这个40万的数字实际上包括了作为寻求庇护者越境的犯罪行为。根据定义，这只能由移民犯下，事实上，这是大多数难民根据合法接受的国际法，实际申请庇护的方式。

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:51.330
<v Damien Mcguinness>Now when you take away that particular criminal act of crossing the border, what you then find is actually-- and the government report very clearly says this-- in fact, the crime rates remained pretty much the same 2015 compared to the year before.
<v Damien Mcguinness>现在当你扣除越境的那种特定刑事行为时，你实际上会发现——政府报告非常清楚地说明了这一点——事实上，2015年的犯罪率与前一年相比基本保持不变。

00:31:51.330 --> 00:32:06.410
<v Brian Reed>The Breitbart article does say that many of these crimes were border crimes. They don't leave that information out completely. But in reality, that's all that 400,000 number is-- immigration infractions like border crossing or overstaying a visa. It doesn't include any other type of crime.
<v Brian Reed>布赖特巴特文章确实说了其中许多犯罪是边境犯罪。他们并没有完全遗漏该信息。但实际上，那40万数字只是——违反移民规定，如越境或签证逾期。它不包括任何其他类型的犯罪。

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:15.580
<v Damien Mcguinness>They're using proper statistics, but not putting them in the right context. And then they're using alarmist language like, "A massive migrant crime wave is surging across Germany."
<v Damien Mcguinness>他们使用正确的统计数据，但没有放在正确的背景下。然后他们使用恐慌性的语言，比如“大规模移民犯罪浪潮正在德国蔓延”。

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:16.590
<v Ben Tyrer>Wow.
<v Ben Tyrer>哇。

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:20.090
<v Brian  Reed>Did you understand that, Ben, from reading this article?
<v Brian  Reed>本，你在阅读这篇文章时理解这一点了吗？

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:21.670
<v Ben Tyrer>Man, I didn't get that at all.
<v Ben Tyrer>伙计，我一点也没明白。

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:28.300
<v Brian Reed>I didn't get it either. I mean, I had to-- I clicked on the link to what they were sourcing, and it's a 135-page report in German.
<v Brian Reed>我也不明白。我是说，我必须——我点击了他们来源的链接，那是一份135页的德语报告。

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:35.780
<v Damien Mcguinness>And it's a complicated report. You know, it's a slog to read through. It's a government report. It's not necessarily very clear itself.
<v Damien Mcguinness>这是一份复杂的报告。你知道，通读它很费劲。这是政府报告。它本身不一定很清楚。

00:32:35.780 --> 00:32:54.990
<v Brian  Reed>So is a migrant crime wave surging across Germany? Damien tells Ben when you take out those crimes like crossing the border or overstaying a visa, the amount of crime committed by immigrants overall has gone up in the last three years. It's nearly tripled, from 3-1/2% of all crimes to 9%.
<v Brian  Reed>那么移民犯罪浪潮是否正在德国蔓延？达米安告诉本，当你除去像越境或逾期居留签证这样的犯罪时，过去三年中移民犯下的犯罪总额确实上升了。它几乎翻了三倍，从占所有犯罪的3.5%上升到9%。

00:32:54.990 --> 00:33:15.130
<v Brian  Reed>Granted, that's at the same time that a huge number of immigrants have been coming into Germany, a million and a half people. But the increase in immigrant crime still appears to be outstripping that population growth. There are caveats to that too, though. The German government has cautioned that the research on this is not yet sophisticated enough to draw firm conclusions. Also, Damien tells Ben--
<v Brian  Reed>诚然，这与大量移民涌入德国的同时发生的，150万人。但移民犯罪的增加似乎仍然超过了人口增长。不过，对此也有告诫。德国政府警告说，对此的研究尚未成熟到足以得出坚定结论的地步。此外，达米安告诉本——

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:20.340
<v Damien Mcguinness>A lot of the rising crime committed by migrants was on other migrants.
<v Damien Mcguinness>许多由移民犯下的日益严重的罪行是针对其他移民的。

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:38.120
<v Brian Reed>According to the interior ministry, migrants were responsible for 4.6% of crimes against German victims and 79% of crimes against immigrant victims. Ben had this picture in his head from the coverage he read of immigrants coming in and victimizing locals. But that's not the reality.
<v Brian Reed>根据内政部的数据，移民应对4.6%的针对德国受害者的犯罪负责，以及79%的针对移民受害者的犯罪。本在读到的报道中脑子里有了这样的画面：移民进来并侵害当地人。但现实并非如此。

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:43.100
<v Damien Mcguinness>It might sound a bit skeptical to say it, but those crimes are not affecting the German population.
<v Damien Mcguinness>说出这一点可能听起来有点怀疑，但这些罪行并没有影响德国人口。

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:56.520
<v Brian Reed>Plus, Damien points out, it's important to bear in mind the situation in which many of these crimes are committed-- in overcrowded, underserved refugee shelters packed with people, many of whom have been traumatized by war and violence in places like Syria and Iraq.
<v Brian Reed>此外，达米安指出，记住许多这些罪行发生的背景非常重要——在人满为患、服务不足的难民收容所，挤满了人，他们中有许多人在叙利亚和伊拉克等地被战争和暴力伤害过。

00:33:56.520 --> 00:34:02.960
<v Damien Mcguinness>So without excusing crime, it's clear that these are stressful conditions in which normal people can make bad choices.
<v Damien Mcguinness>因此，在不原谅犯罪的情况下，很明显这些是充满压力的条件，普通人在这种条件下可能会做出错误的选择。

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:20.020
<v Brian Reed>We talked through a few other things Ben found in his research. I tell him that a French speaking colleague and I spoke to several law enforcement sources in France, and they all said the concept of "no-go zones," immigrant neighborhoods where native French people were supposedly not allowed in, are not real.
<v Brian Reed>我们讨论了本在研究中发现的其他几件事。我告诉他，一位讲法语的同事和我与法国的几位执法消息来源谈过，他们都说“禁区”这个概念，即据说法国本地人不允许进入的移民社区，是不存在的。

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:41.150
<v Brian Reed>One of them told us it was, quote, "an American invention." And Damien agrees. He says it's become a bit of a joke in Europe that to Americans, entire cities are no-go zones. Damien also tells Ben about a Breitbart story he had personal experience with from earlier this year, which claimed that a mob of Muslim men had set Germany's oldest church on fire.
<v Brian Reed>其中一人告诉我们它是“美国人的发明”。达米安也表示同意。他说在欧洲，美国人认为整个城市都是禁区已经成为一个笑话。达米安还告诉本一个他亲身经历的、今年早些时候的布赖特巴特故事，声称一群穆斯林男子放火烧毁了德国最古老的教堂。

00:34:41.150 --> 00:35:09.340
<v Brian Reed>It included a video of supposed Islamists chanting in the streets nearby and celebrating. Damien remembers seeing that story appear on the internet and then trying to run it down with other journalists, only to find that the church was not Germany's oldest. It had not been set on fire. A stray firework had briefly ignited some netting on some scaffolding, but that was it. And the men celebrating in the video were not Islamists, but actually anti-Islamist. They were Syrian refugees celebrating a ceasefire in the war back home.
<v Brian Reed>它包含了一段视频，据说是伊斯兰主义者在附近街道上高呼口号并庆祝。达米安记得看到那个故事出现在网上，然后试图和其他记者一起去追查，结果发现那座教堂并不是德国最古老的。它并没有被放火烧毁。一枚流弹烟花暂时点燃了脚手架上的一些网，仅此而已。视频中庆祝的人不是伊斯兰主义者，实际上是反伊斯兰主义者。他们是叙利亚难民在庆祝家乡战争停火。

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:11.970
<v Ben Tyrer>I remember that story. My gosh.
<v Ben Tyrer>我记得那个故事。天哪。

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:13.610
<v Damien Mcguinness>You remember reading it?
<v Damien Mcguinness>你记得读过它？

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:15.000
<v Ben Tyrer>Yes.
<v Ben Tyrer>是的，确实记得。

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:19.620
<v Brian  Reed>It was something Ben came across in his research and he believed it.
<v Brian  Reed>这是本在研究中遇到的事情，并且他相信了。

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:23.850
<v Brian Reed>So you thought that Islamists burned down Germany's oldest church?
<v Brian Reed>所以你认为伊斯兰主义者烧毁了德国最古老的教堂？

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:35.650
<v Ben Tyrer>Yes, yes I did. I mean, I had no reason not to. Wow. I'm kind of flabbergasted, actually, and more than a little bit angry at this point.
<v Ben Tyrer>是的，是的，我确实那么认为。我是说，我没有理由不相信。哇。我实际上有点目瞪口呆，而且现在感到不止一点点的愤怒。

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:58.300
<v Brian  Reed>And lastly, we come to the document that helped clinch Ben's decision for him-- his decision that he was opposed to the resolution in Homer. It's that report by the think tank the Gatestone Institute, called The Islamization of Germany, which lists 19 pages of crimes, all linked in some way to Muslims and migrants.
<v Brian  Reed>最后，我们谈到了那份帮助巩固本决定的文件——他决定反对荷马市那项决议。就是那份由智库盖特斯通研究所撰写的报告，标题是《德国的伊斯兰化》，列出了19页的罪行，全部以某种方式与穆斯林和移民联系在一起。

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:12.220
<v Damien Mcguinness>Yeah, I'm flicking through the report now. And, you know, if I just take one example. There's a crime on July 22nd, 2016. So I'm reading it now. It says Ali Sonboly, you know, Muslim name--
<v Damien Mcguinness>是的，我正在翻阅这份报告。而且，你知道，如果我只举一个例子。有一个罪行是2016年7月22日。所以我现在正在读它。它说阿里·松博利（Ali Sonboly），你知道，穆斯林名字——

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:27.600
<v Brian Reed>Ali Sonboly, it reads. Quote, "An 18-year-old Iranian German who harbored hatred for Arabs and Turks killed 10 people, including himself, and wounded 35 others at a McDonald's in Munich." That's the whole entry.
<v Brian Reed>“阿里·松博利，”它是这样读的。引述：“一名18岁的伊朗裔德国人，怀着对阿拉伯人和土耳其人的仇恨，在慕尼黑一家麦当劳杀害了10人，包括他自己，并造成35人受伤。”这就是整条条目。

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:46.400
<v Brian Reed>Damien reported on this story as it happened. And first of all, he tells Ben, when it says Sonboly was, quote, "Iranian-German," he was born to Iranian parents, but in Germany. He grew up in Germany, spoke German. He was German. And as for including Sonboly in a report called The Islamization of Germany--
<v Brian Reed>达米安在故事发生时就报道了它。首先，他告诉本，当它说松博利是“伊朗裔德国人”时，他出生于伊朗父母，但在德国。他在德国长大，说德语。他是德国人。至于把松博利包括在一份名为《德国的伊斯兰化》的报告中——

00:36:46.400 --> 00:37:03.960
<v Damien Mcguinness>In fact, it was found later that he had large amounts of neo-Nazi memorabilia. He was a great fan of the Norwegian mass murderer Breivik-- you might remember killed all those young people in Norway.
<v Damien Mcguinness>事实上，后来发现他有大量的新纳粹纪念品。他是挪威大规模杀人犯布雷维克（Breivik）的忠实粉丝——你可能记得他在挪威杀害了所有那些年轻人。

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:23.080
<v Damien Mcguinness>He was reportedly very proud of having the same birthday as-- I can't remember if it was Adolf Hitler, or if it was the same birthday as Breivik. And he hated foreigners. Ali Sonboly was not an Islamist. He was a right wing extremist.
<v Damien Mcguinness>据报道，他非常自豪能与阿道夫·希特勒，或者是与布雷维克同一天生日。他仇恨外国人。阿里·松博利不是伊斯兰主义者。他是一个右翼极端分子。

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:25.980
<v Brian Reed>Most of Sonboly's victims were foreigners.
<v Brian Reed>松博利的大多数受害者是外国人。

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:31.710
<v Damien Mcguinness>This should be in a report about anti-foreigner, right wing extremist violence. It's in the wrong report.
<v Damien Mcguinness>这应该出现在一份关于反外国人、右翼极端暴力行为的报告中。它出现在了错误的报告里。

00:37:31.710 --> 00:38:01.030
<v Brian Reed>Damien says since 1991, Islamist attacks have caused 14 deaths in Germany. In the same time period, he says, there have been approximately 150 deaths at the hands of right wing extremists in the country. And according to the government, right wing violence increased dramatically as the big wave of migrants started coming in. But Ben came across none of this in his research.
<v Brian Reed>达米安说，自1991年以来，伊斯兰主义者的袭击在德国造成了14人死亡。在同一时期，他说，该国大约有150人死于右翼极端分子之手。而且根据政府的数据，随着大规模移民开始涌入，右翼暴力急剧增加。但本在研究中根本没有遇到任何这些信息。

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:05.790
<v Brian Reed>I wondered what Ben was thinking during all this. And I was surprised to hear him say this.
<v Brian Reed>我想知道本在这一切中在想什么。听到他这么说，我很惊讶。

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:10.140
<v Ben Tyrer>It's definitely making me lean toward the thought that I was wrong.
<v Ben Tyrer>这绝对让我倾向于认为我是错的。

00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:10.710
<v Brian Reed>Really?
<v Brian Reed>真的吗？

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:11.890
<v Ben Tyrer>Yeah.
<v Ben Tyrer>是的。

00:38:11.890 --> 00:38:14.730
<v Brian Reed>You think you were like wrong wrong?
<v Brian Reed>你认为你错了？

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:19.240
<v Ben Tyrer>Yes. I mean, not through lack of trying. But I think I was wrong.
<v Ben Tyrer>是的。我是说，不是我不努力。但我认为我是错的。

00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:33.620
<v Brian Reed>He thinks he was wrong in his conclusion that immigration makes places more unsafe. Ben meant so well, tried so hard to be skeptical, to be fact based, to find the truth, and still he feels he was led astray.
<v Brian Reed>他认为他在移民使地方变得更不安全的结论上错了。本出于好意，非常努力地去保持怀疑，去以事实为基础，去寻找真相，然而他仍然感到被误导了。

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:58.980
<v Brian Reed>A lot of that he chalks up to him being such a novice to news. He says it seems to him that the sources he was reading, like Breitbart and the report from the Gatestone Institute-- which is a right wing think tank, chaired by former U.N. Ambassador under George W. Bush, John Bolton-- were successful in doing what they were designed to do. Sway him against immigration. But it's also true that Ben took himself to those sources.
<v Brian Reed>其中很多原因归结于他是一个新闻初学者。他说在他看来，他正在阅读的来源，比如布赖特巴特和盖特斯通研究所的报告——那是一家右翼智库，由乔治·W·布什时期的前联合国大使约翰·博尔顿（John Bolton）担任主席——成功地做了它们被设计用来做的事情。让他反对移民。但本自己去寻找这些来源也是事实。

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:16.990
<v Ben Tyrer>I think the conclusion I've come to is that I was picking this information. And part of it was irrational fear. I admit that.
<v Ben Tyrer>我想我得出的结论是，我是在挑选这些信息。其中一部分是无理的恐惧。我承认这一点。

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:41.230
<v Ben Tyrer>I wouldn't have put so much time into it if I hadn't been afraid. I think the fear just kind of fed on itself. I mean, I told you before, I try to be unemotional about this stuff, but the more I think about it, the more I think that I may-- that I was more emotional than I like to let myself believe. And it skewed my thinking.
<v Ben Tyrer>如果我不是因为害怕，就不会投入这么多时间。我想恐惧只是在喂养自身。我是说，我之前告诉过你，我试着对这些东西保持冷漠，但我想得越多，我就越觉得——我当时比我喜欢让自己相信的要情绪化得多。这扭曲了我的思维。

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:59.090
<v Brian Reed>Ben was not the only person in Homer who brought up Europe and specifically Germany with me, as we were discussing immigration. This is the weird moment the world is in right now, where people at the end of the road in Alaska are examining Germany to decide their feelings on a local city resolution.
<v Brian Reed>本并不是荷马市唯一一个和我讨论移民问题时提到欧洲、特别是德国的人。这就是当今世界奇怪的时刻，阿拉斯加道路尽头的人们正在审查德国，来决定他们对本地城市决议的感受。

00:39:59.090 --> 00:40:16.330
<v Brian Reed>While Ben does feel he was wrong to fear immigration, he doesn't think he was wrong to encourage his city government to be cautious if it were to invite a lot of immigrants in. He wants to have open arms. But he still believes it's important to have a plan in place for everyone's safety. And he's still not sure what the right way to do that is.
