00:50:26.740 --> 00:50:43.640
<v State Attorney>Due to the lack of corroboration and substantial inconsistencies from the facts that we have obtained from multiple sources, the original charges cannot be sustained and prosecution on those charges is untenable.
<v State Attorney>由于缺乏确证，并且从我们从多个来源获得的事实中存在实质性矛盾，原始指控无法维持，对这些指控的起诉是站不住脚的。

00:50:43.640 --> 00:51:01.890
<v Zoe Chace>The prosecution couldn't back up the girl's narrative of events. She said she was forced into the bathroom. But that wasn't backed up on the videotape from the hallway. She said she didn't know the boys. But she had been sexting with one of them the night before. The texts showed they planned to meet in the bathroom.
<v Zoe Chace>检方无法支持这个女孩对事件的陈述。她说她被迫进入洗手间。但走廊里的录像并没有支持这一点。她说她不认识这些男孩。但前一天晚上她一直在和其中一个发色情短信。短信显示他们计划在洗手间见面。

00:51:01.890 --> 00:51:25.850
<v Zoe Chace>I cannot tell you if this girl was raped. All I can say is that the lawyers say the incident didn't have the evidence to support her claim of rape in a court of law. It can also say that in a high school of teenagers, two older boys having sex with a younger girl, a 14-year-old girl, in a bathroom at 9:00 in the morning-- it's worrisome, any way you look at it.
<v Zoe Chace>我不能告诉你这个女孩是否被强奸了。我只能说律师们说该事件没有证据在法庭上支持她关于强奸的说法。它也可以说，在青少年的高中里，两个大一点的男孩在早上9点在洗手间和一个年轻女孩，一个14岁的女孩发生性关系——无论你从哪个角度看，这都是令人担心的。

00:51:25.850 --> 00:51:46.900
<v Zoe Chace>I feel upset talking about it. I felt upset hearing about it. I tried reaching out to the girl through the law firm that was representing her multiple times, but they didn't respond. Under Maryland law, these kids are too close in age for this to be statutory rape. The state did decide to charge the boys with something else-- child pornography.
<v Zoe Chace>谈论它让我感到心烦。听到它让我感到心烦。我多次尝试通过代表她的律师事务所联系这个女孩，但他们没有回应。根据马里兰州法律，这些孩子年龄太接近，不属于法定强奸。州政府确实决定以其他罪名指控这些男孩——儿童色情罪。

00:51:46.900 --> 00:52:02.940
<v Zoe Chace>The girl had sent one of the boys naked pictures and videos of herself, and he forwarded them on to the other kid. So Henry, who's 18, remains in jail. Jose, who's 17, was released, and then picked up by ICE that same day. He's now in the custody of Immigration Enforcement.
<v Zoe Chace>那个女孩给其中一个男孩发了她自己的裸体照片和视频，他把它们转发给了另一个孩子。所以18岁的亨利仍被关在监狱里。17岁的何塞被释放了，然后当天就被ICE抓走了。他现在处于移民执法局的监护下。

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:25.480
<v Zoe Chace>I wanted to talk to someone who took this dark, complicated situation involving teenagers and decided the most important thing about it was what it said about our immigration system. The people whose reporting unleashed a flood of threats on that school. I wanted to ask, why do that? I wanted to hear their rationale-- like, what were they trying to achieve?
<v Zoe Chace>我想和一个把这种黑暗、复杂、涉及青少年的情况拿出来，并决定关于它的最重要的事情是它说明了我们的移民制度的人交谈。那些报道引发了学校威胁潮的人。我想问，为什么要这样做？我想听听他们的理由——比如，他们想实现什么？

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:29.690
<v Zoe Chace>And I asked Tucker Carlson. He turned me down for an interview. But I got someone close.
<v Zoe Chace>我问了塔克·卡尔森。他拒绝了我的采访。但我找到了一个接近的人。

00:52:29.690 --> 00:52:39.730
<v Ann Coulter>We are talking about cultures that are just behind ours. They are backwards. They're behind us in tolerance, eating with a knife and fork.
<v Ann Coulter>我们谈论的是那些落后于我们的文化。他们是落后的。他们在宽容度、用刀叉吃饭方面落后于我们。

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:57.870
<v Zoe Chace>Ann Coulter. Yeah, I called Ann Coulter. Notorious right wing columnist, major influencer behind Trump's immigration policies during the campaign. She'd written a column about the case right after the charges were dropped, titled, "To Say, 'Stop Raping Me!' In English, Press '1' Now."
<v Zoe Chace>安·库尔特（Ann Coulter）。是的，我打了安·库尔特的电话。臭名昭著的右翼专栏作家，特朗普竞选期间移民政策背后的主要影响者。在指控撤销后，她写了一篇关于此案的专栏，标题是《现在要用英语说“停止强奸我！”，按“1”》。

00:52:57.870 --> 00:53:10.740
<v Zoe Chace>Her book, Adios America, has multiple chapters focusing on sexual assault cases committed by undocumented immigrants. And to anyone who would call it racist or xenophobic to make the Rockville case about immigration, she says--
<v Zoe Chace>她的书，《再见美国》（Adios America），有多个章节专门关注无证移民犯下的性侵案。对于任何称将罗克维尔案件与移民联系起来是种族主义或排外的人，她说——

00:53:10.740 --> 00:53:13.130
<v Ann Coulter>Yes, I'm instantly saying F you.
<v Ann Coulter>是的，我立刻要说“去你的”。

00:53:13.130 --> 00:53:23.130
<v Zoe Chace>This is her reaction when I show her a letter the superintendent Jack Smith sent home to parents, which includes a line condemning racist and xenophobic threats against students.
<v Zoe Chace>这是当我给她看校长杰克·史密斯发给家长的信时她的反应，其中包含一行谴责针对学生的种族主义和排外威胁。

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:25.950
<v Ann  Coulter>Oh, [BLEEP] him.
<v Ann  Coulter>哦，[哔]他。

00:53:25.950 --> 00:53:27.470
<v Zoe Chace>So what's the part?
<v Zoe Chace>那部分是什么？

00:53:27.470 --> 00:53:40.030
<v Ann  Coulter>While many of you have engaged in-- crossed the line with racist, xenophobic calls and emails-- oh, boo hoo hoo. A 14-year-old girl was at least statutorily gang raped.
<v Ann  Coulter>“虽然你们中的许多人参与了——越过了种族主义、排外电话和邮件的界线——哦，呜呜呜。一个14岁的女孩至少在法定上被轮奸了。”

00:53:40.030 --> 00:53:45.080
<v Zoe Chace>But the racist, xenophobic calls also affect other students in the school.
<v Zoe Chace>但种族主义、排外电话也影响了学校里的其他学生。

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:59.640
<v Ann  Coulter>I think now is not the time to be discussing that. Right now, a girl has been raped. People are angry about it. And they are expressing anger. And he turns around and attacks the people expressing anger by calling them names.
<v Ann  Coulter>我认为现在不是讨论那个的时候。现在，一个女孩被强奸了。人们对此很愤怒。他们正在表达愤怒。他转过身来，通过叫他们的名字来攻击表达愤怒的人。

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.580
<v Zoe Chace>When do you want to hear about xenophobia?
<v Zoe Chace>你什么时候想听关于排外的问题？

00:54:01.580 --> 00:54:05.460
<v Ann Coulter>When it's a problem. Right now, illegal immigration's a problem.
<v Ann Coulter>当它是个问题的时候。现在，非法移民是个问题。

00:54:05.460 --> 00:54:15.080
<v Zoe Chace>Ann does not believe the prosecutor should have dropped the charges. She doesn't believe the evidence is ambiguous or problematic. She believes unequivocally this was rape.
<v Zoe Chace>安不认为检察官应该撤销指控。她不认为证据是模棱两可或成问题的。她毫不含糊地认为这就是强奸。

00:54:15.080 --> 00:54:24.900
<v Zoe Chace>I played Ann some of the voicemails that poured into the school, after her and others' coverage of the incident. Was that the response she was hoping for? Or was she at least OK with that response?
<v Zoe Chace>我给安播放了她和其他人报道该事件后涌入学校的一些语音留言。那是她所希望的反应吗？或者她至少对那个反应感到满意吗？

00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:26.960
<v Voicemail Recording>7:59 AM.
<v Voicemail Recording>早上7:59。

00:54:26.960 --> 00:54:33.810
<v Male Caller To Voicemail>Too bad you didn't rape the mother [BLEEP] who's responsible to let these mother [BLEEP] in.
<v Male Caller To Voicemail>太糟糕了，你没有强奸那个负责让这些母亲[哔]进来的人。

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:38.440
<v Zoe Chace>Ann reaches for her nicotine gum and considers this. She is unsurprised.
<v Zoe Chace>安拿了她的尼古丁口香糖，考虑了一下。她并不感到意外。

00:54:38.440 --> 00:55:04.970
<v Ann Coulter>I think what you're hearing in the anger here-- it is the frustration and the feeling that there's nothing you can do. And frankly, it's going to get worse because they just put a completely implausible reality TV star in the White House hoping to end it. And so far, he's not ending it. If he betrays them, oh, my gosh, you're going to get a lot more messages like that.
<v Ann Coulter>我认为你在这里听到的愤怒——是挫折感和那种你无能为力的感觉。坦率地说，这会变得更糟，因为他们刚刚把一个完全荒谬的真人秀明星送进白宫，希望结束这一切。到目前为止，他并没有结束它。如果他背叛他们，哦，天哪，你会收到更多那样的信息。

00:55:04.970 --> 00:55:15.130
<v Zoe Chace>Basically, if Trump doesn't crack down on immigration like he said he would, the Jack Smiths of the world are going to get a lot more calls like this. Her message to them-- stop whining.
<v Zoe Chace>基本上，如果特朗普不像他说的那样打击移民，像杰克·史密斯这样的人会收到更多这样的电话。她对他们的信息——别发牢骚了。

00:55:15.130 --> 00:55:18.260
<v Ann Coulter>Now they know what it feels like to be Ann Coulter for a day.
<v Ann Coulter>现在他们知道当一天安·库尔特是什么感觉了。

00:55:18.260 --> 00:55:22.070
<v Zoe Chace>I get the feeling that maybe Ann Coulter gets a lot of death threats in her line of work.
<v Zoe Chace>我有种感觉，也许安·库尔特在她的工作岗位上收到了很多死亡威胁。

00:55:22.070 --> 00:55:32.550
<v Ann Coulter>It's gone on with me long enough that I can tell you, well, not only have I never whined in public about it, but if they call first, they're not coming. These are just people leaving nasty messages.
<v Ann Coulter>这发生在我身上已经够久了，我可以告诉你，不仅我从来没有在公开场合发过牢骚，而且如果他们先打电话，他们就不会来了。这些只是留下令人讨厌信息的人。

00:55:32.550 --> 00:55:42.880
<v Zoe Chace>Well, people said they were going to come and shoot up the school because there were so many illegal immigrants there. I mean, that was a threat that was being made at the school.
<v Zoe Chace>嗯，人们说他们要来学校扫射，因为那里有这么多非法移民。我是说，那是学校面临的一个威胁。

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:51.210
<v Ann Coulter>OK, some nut makes a phone call. I think the real issue here is we have illegal immigrants raping 14-year-old girls-- allegedly. [LAUGHS]
<v Ann Coulter>好吧，某个疯子打了个电话。我认为这里真正的问题是我们有非法移民在强奸14岁的女孩——据称。[笑]

00:55:51.210 --> 00:55:53.610
<v Zoe  Chace>Thank you.
<v Zoe  Chace>谢谢。

00:55:53.610 --> 00:56:16.280
<v Zoe  Chace>We talked for two and a half hours, and she helped me understand why she wrote about this-- why she thinks it was necessary and important to turn this incident into a case study about the evils of immigration. And her explanation surprised me. She said she and Tucker Carlson, they're not saying this stuff to make people angry.
<v Zoe  Chace>我们谈了两个半小时，她帮助我理解了她为什么要写这个——为什么她认为把这起事件变成一个关于移民罪恶的案例研究是必要和重要的。她的解释让我感到惊讶。她说她和塔克·卡尔森，他们说这些东西并不是为了让人们生气。

00:56:16.280 --> 00:56:47.760
<v Zoe  Chace>People are already angry, she says, because no one in power is listening to them. Not the schools, not the policy makers, definitely not the media. So Ann's columns-- her screaming angry columns-- that's not trying to rile those people up. That's Ann screaming at the rest of the media for ignoring the fact that so many people are so angry, she thinks. She's screaming because the rest of the media is not. And she says what we're hearing on the school's voicemails is people's anger over this.
<v Zoe  Chace>人们已经很生气了，她说，因为掌权的人没有人听他们说。不是学校，不是政策制定者，绝对不是媒体。所以安的专栏——她那尖叫的愤怒专栏——那不是为了煽动那些人。那是安在对媒体的其他部分尖叫，因为他们忽视了这么多人如此生气的事实。她尖叫是因为媒体的其他部分没有。她说我们在学校语音留言中听到的是人们对此的愤怒。

00:56:47.760 --> 00:56:58.380
<v Ann Coulter>They are getting very, very frustrated. And no one is listening to them. And the media is sneering at them and hiding stories that are important to their lives.
<v Ann Coulter>他们正变得非常、非常沮丧。没有人听他们说。媒体在嘲笑他们，并隐瞒对他们的生活很重要的故事。

00:56:58.380 --> 00:57:15.960
<v Zoe Chace>I don't think, though, that the callers are frustrated that the mainstream media is not reporting on it, which I think you are. The callers are antagonized, or whipped up by what Tucker's reporting, and calling the school, and threatening the superintendent. That's what it seems like the order of operations was.
<v Zoe Chace>虽然我不认为，打来电话的人是因为主流媒体没有报道而感到沮丧，虽然我认为你是这样。打来电话的人是被塔克的报道激怒或煽动，然后打电话给学校，威胁校长。那看起来是操作顺序。

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:28.200
<v Ann Coulter>Well, it's all a part of the same thing-- ignoring the problem of illegal immigration, whether it's the immigration bureaucracy, the schools employing them, the taxes going to pay for them. I mean, in some ways the media is a safety valve.
<v Ann Coulter>好吧，这一切都是同一件事的一部分——忽视非法移民的问题，无论是移民官僚机构、雇用他们的学校，还是去支付他们的税收。我是说，在某些方面，媒体是一个安全阀。

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:53.790
<v Zoe Chace>The media as a safety valve. Ann thinks if the media reflected the anger of people who are upset about illegal immigration, then those people would be less virulent with their threats, and their voicemails, and their emails, and their Facebook posts. Like this is how she thinks the news should look. Take the very first Washington Post story about this which ran in the metro section with the headline-- Two Students Charged After Rape at Maryland High School.
<v Zoe Chace>媒体作为安全阀。安认为如果媒体反映了那些对非法移民感到不满的人的愤怒，那么这些人就不会对他们的威胁、语音留言、电子邮件和Facebook帖子那么刻毒。比如这看起来应该是新闻的样子。看看第一篇报道这件事的《华盛顿邮报》文章，它登在都市版，标题是——两名学生在马里兰高中强奸后被指控。

00:57:53.790 --> 00:57:58.470
<v Zoe Chace>What should the Washington Post have said if you could have been the editor of that story?
<v Zoe Chace>如果你能成为那个故事的编辑，你应该怎么说？

00:57:58.470 --> 00:58:00.290
<v Ann Coulter>I would have put it on the front page!
<v Ann Coulter>我会把它放在头版！

00:58:00.290 --> 00:58:02.470
<v Zoe Chace>And you would have said? What would the headline...
<v Zoe Chace>你会说？标题会是什么……

00:58:02.470 --> 00:58:27.600
<v Ann Coulter>Immigration policy implicated in the suburbs. Or implicated in rape in the suburbs...implication or immigration seen as factor...no, as cause! They wouldn't have been here if we had a wall. Another argument for Trump's wall.
<v Ann Coulter>移民政策牵涉到郊区。或者牵涉到郊区的强奸……牵涉或移民被视为因素……不，作为起因！如果我们有一堵墙，他们就不会在这儿了。这是特朗普之墙的另一个论据。

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:32.610
<v Zoe Chace>Hm. But then being immigrants doesn't necessarily have to do with the raping.
<v Zoe Chace>嗯。但身为移民不一定非要和强奸有关。

00:58:32.610 --> 00:58:36.060
<v Ann Coulter>Who cares. These rapists would not have been here.
<v Ann Coulter>谁在乎。这些强奸犯本不会在这里的。

00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:44.350
<v Zoe  Chace>I just thought that it was so much more about immigration policy, and it really seems like it's a media criticism.
<v Zoe  Chace>我只是觉得这更多是关于移民政策，而这看起来确实是对媒体的批评。

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:50.240
<v Ann Coulter>Yes, over their defense of illegal immigration.
<v Ann Coulter>是的，因为他们对非法移民的辩护。

00:58:50.240 --> 00:59:17.800
<v Zoe Chace>There is no evidence that being born somewhere else leads people to commit more crimes than if they were born right here in the US. The numbers that exist just don't prove that out. There are all these fears being whipped up around the country about this perceived national menace, but inside Rockville High School, there were fears just about walking down the hallway.
<v Zoe Chace>没有证据表明出生在其他地方的人比出生在美国的人更容易犯罪。现有的数字并没有证明这一点。全国到处都是关于这种感知到的国家威胁的恐惧，但在罗克维尔高中内部，有仅仅关于走在走廊里的恐惧。

00:59:17.800 --> 00:59:26.250
<v Female Student>I guess in the point of view of a girl, the girls felt unsafe. You know, they didn't want to go to the bathroom. They're like I'm scared now.
<v Female Student>我猜站在女孩的角度，女孩们感到不安全。你知道，她们不想去洗手间。她们像是我现在很害怕。

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:32.910
<v Zoe Chace>This is Keydie, ninth grader at Rockville High School. She's Hispanic. Her parents are from Honduras.
<v Zoe Chace>这是基迪（Keydie），罗克维尔高中的九年级学生。她是西班牙裔。她的父母来自洪都拉斯。

00:59:32.910 --> 00:59:53.730
<v Zoe Chace>She's one of those girls who seems like she just came into her own, like recently transitioned over from kid to young adult. In the weeks and months since the alleged rape in the bathroom, and the national firestorm that came after it, the fears of her and her classmates took on different forms. She felt scared as a Honduran American, and as the daughter of immigrants.
<v Zoe Chace>她是那些看起来刚刚成长起来的女孩之一，就像最近从孩子过渡到年轻成年人一样。在洗手间涉嫌强奸案以及随之而来的全国性风暴之后的几周和几个月里，她和同学们的恐惧以不同的形式出现。她作为一个洪都拉斯裔美国人，和一个移民的女儿，感到害怕。

00:59:53.730 --> 01:00:23.070
<v Keydie>And then, for the immigrant-- I guess the Hispanic students in our school-- they were at a point where they were feeling judged and scared, because they were thinking that the guy who did this was being more accused due to the fact that he's Hispanic, you know? He's being judged even more. Like, if we do something now, they're going to kick us out of the school or something, because we don't have papers.
<v Keydie>然后，对于移民——我猜是我们学校的西班牙裔学生——他们处于一种感到被评判和害怕的状态，因为他们认为做这件事的人仅仅因为他是西班牙裔而被更多地指责，你知道吗？他被评判得更多。比如，如果我们现在做什么，他们会把我们踢出学校什么的，因为我们没有证件。

01:00:23.070 --> 01:00:25.290
<v Zoe Chace>And she felt scared as a girl.
<v Zoe Chace>而且她作为一个女孩感到害怕。

01:00:25.290 --> 01:00:43.640
<v Keydie>I was scared-- what if something like that could happen to me. Because I don't know all the people in my school. Like, I don't know their intentions. Like, they could seem nice, but I don't know if they want to have a nice friendship and whatnot. Like, you don't know what to expect. Like, you just can't be trusting people like that.
<v Keydie>我很害怕——如果那样的事情发生在我身上怎么办。因为我不认识学校里的所有人。比如，我不知道他们的意图。比如，他们看起来可能很友好，但我不知道他们是否想有很好的友谊什么的。比如，你不知道会发生什么。比如，你就是不能那样信任人。

01:00:43.640 --> 01:00:47.700
<v Zoe Chace>It's like all the students were afraid for different reasons at the same time.
<v Zoe Chace>就像所有的学生同时因为不同的原因感到害怕一样。

01:00:47.700 --> 01:00:48.410
<v Keydie>Exactly.
<v Keydie>正是如此。

01:00:48.410 --> 01:00:55.710
<v Zoe Chace>That's lots of people right now. Lots of people are afraid for different reasons at the same time.
<v Zoe Chace>现在很多人都是这样。很多人同时因为不同的原因感到害怕。

01:00:55.710 --> 01:01:16.280
<v Ira  Glass>Zoe Chace is one of the producers of our show.
